---
import Developers from "~/layouts/Developers.astro";
import { DEMO, DEMO_SOURCE } from "~/data/developers";

const documentExample = `{
  "client_id": "https://shop.example/oauth-client-metadata.json",
  "client_name": "Example Shop",
  "client_uri": "https://shop.example",
  "logo_uri": "https://shop.example/logo.png",
  "application_type": "web",
  "redirect_uris": ["https://shop.example/callback"],
  "grant_types": ["authorization_code"],
  "response_types": ["code"],
  "scope": "openid email profile",
  "token_endpoint_auth_method": "private_key_jwt",
  "jwks_uri": "https://shop.example/jwks.json",
  "brigid_attributes": { "email": "required", "given_name": "optional" }
}`;

const authorizeExample = `https://id.example.org/authorize
  ?response_type=code
  &client_id=https%3A%2F%2Fshop.example%2Foauth-client-metadata.json
  &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fshop.example%2Fcallback
  &scope=openid%20email
  &state=…&nonce=…
  &code_challenge=…&code_challenge_method=S256`;

const tokenExample = `POST https://id.example.org/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=authorization_code
&code=…
&redirect_uri=https://shop.example/callback
&code_verifier=…
&client_id=https://shop.example/oauth-client-metadata.json
&client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer
&client_assertion=eyJhbGciOiJFUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6InNob3AtMSJ9…`;

const assertionExample = `header  { "alg": "ES256", "kid": "shop-1" }
payload {
  "iss": "https://shop.example/oauth-client-metadata.json",
  "sub": "https://shop.example/oauth-client-metadata.json",
  "aud": "https://id.example.org",
  "jti": "a fresh random value",
  "iat": 1795212000,
  "exp": 1795212060
}`;

const fields = [
  [
    "client_id",
    "The address of the document itself, exactly. It is your site's identifier on every server.",
  ],
  [
    "client_name",
    "Shown to people beside your domain, never instead of it: anyone could write any name.",
  ],
  [
    "redirect_uris",
    "Where people come back. https, on the same origin as client_id, compared exactly.",
  ],
  [
    "token_endpoint_auth_method",
    "private_key_jwt for a site with a server, none for a page in the browser. Never a client secret.",
  ],
  [
    "jwks_uri or jwks",
    "With private_key_jwt: your public keys (Ed25519 or P-256), at an address or in the document, not both.",
  ],
  [
    "brigid_attributes",
    "What you ask people to share, each attribute required or optional. Without it, you receive an identifier and nothing else.",
  ],
  ["logo_uri, client_uri", "Optional. https only."],
];
---

<Developers
  title="Sign in on a website · Developers"
  description="Add sign-in with brig·id to a website: publish a document that describes it, find the person's server, run the code flow with PKCE."
  lead="Sign in on a website,"
  accent="registered nowhere."
  intro="Three steps: publish a document that describes your site, find the person's server, run the Authorization Code flow. Most OpenID Connect libraries do the third for you."
>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>What your site receives</h2>
    <p>
      A person signs in with one of their <strong>identities</strong>, the one
      they choose for your site. You receive an identifier made for that
      identity and your site only (<code>sub</code>), the server that vouches
      for it (<code>iss</code>), and the attributes you asked for that the
      person accepted to share, value by value. Nothing else: not their other
      identities, not where else they sign in.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>
        Know a person by <code>iss</code> and <code>sub</code> together
      </strong>
      , never by <code>sub</code> alone and never by an e-mail address. A server
      can only speak for its own people: with this rule, no server can sign in
      to your site as someone from another one.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Publish a document that describes your site</h2>
    <p>
      Put a JSON document on your own domain, at an https address with a path.
      We suggest <code>/oauth-client-metadata.json</code>. Its address is your
      <code>client_id</code>. This is the OAuth Client ID Metadata Document, the
      same mechanism Bluesky and the Model Context Protocol use.
    </p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code rise"><code>{documentExample}</code></pre>

  <dl class="fields rise">
    {fields.map(([name, text]) => (
      <div>
        <dt>
          <code>{name}</code>
        </dt>
        <dd>{text}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      <strong>Choose the address once.</strong> The identifier each person gets
      is made from it: changing it changes everyone's identifier. The document
      itself can change at any time; servers keep it at most a day, or what your
      <code>Cache-Control</code> header says, and no less than five minutes.
      Keep it under 5 KiB, serve it over https without redirects, as
      <code>application/json</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A server's operator decides which documents it accepts: any (the default),
      only the ones they accept, or only the sites registered on it. They can
      also cap what any such site may ask for. A request beyond the cap is
      refused with <code>invalid_scope</code>, as one beyond your document.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Find the person's server</h2>
    <p>
      Ask for the domain of their server, the way an e-mail service asks for an
      address: <code>id.example.org</code>. Read
      <code>https://id.example.org/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>: it
      gives the endpoints, and{" "}
      <code>client_id_metadata_document_supported: true</code>
      when the server accepts sites that describe themselves. Remember which
      server each sign-in went to: the answer must come from it.
    </p>

    <h2>3. The Authorization Code flow, with PKCE</h2>
    <p>
      Send the person to the server's authorization endpoint. PKCE with
      <code>S256</code> is required for every site; <code>state</code> and
      <code>nonce</code> as usual. Ask for <code>openid</code>, and for
      <code>email</code> or <code>profile</code> to ask for what your document
      declares. <code>prompt=select_account</code> asks the server to show the
      choice of identity even when one is remembered.
    </p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code rise"><code>{authorizeExample}</code></pre>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      The person comes back with a <code>code</code>, your <code>state</code>
      and the server's <code>iss</code> (RFC 9207): check both. Exchange the
      code at the token endpoint with the PKCE verifier, and prove who you are.
    </p>
    <h3>
      A site with a server: <code>private_key_jwt</code>
    </h3>
    <p>
      Sign a short JWT with your private key, EdDSA (Ed25519) or ES256 (P-256),
      whose public half your document publishes. <code>iss</code> and
      <code>sub</code> are your <code>client_id</code>, <code>aud</code> the
      server's issuer (its token endpoint works too), a fresh <code>jti</code>,
      valid a few minutes, ten at most. A server refuses an assertion it has
      seen before. To rotate keys, publish the new one, then sign with it: a
      server that does not know a <code>kid</code> reads your keys again.
    </p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code rise"><code>{assertionExample}</code></pre>
  <pre class="code rise"><code>{tokenExample}</code></pre>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <h3>
      A page in the browser: <code>none</code>
    </h3>
    <p>
      Declare <code>token_endpoint_auth_method: "none"</code> and send only your
      <code>client_id</code>. PKCE is what proves the page that asked is the one
      asking. The token endpoint and the keys answer any origin.
    </p>

    <h3>Then</h3>
    <p>
      You receive an ID token signed with EdDSA. Check it against the server's
      keys (<code>jwks_uri</code>): signature, <code>iss</code> (the server you
      sent the person to), <code>aud</code> (your <code>client_id</code>),
      <code>nonce</code>, expiry. A declined optional attribute and one the
      identity does not have look the same: absent.
    </p>

    <h2>Libraries</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing here is specific to brig·id. The demo uses
      <a href="https://github.com/panva/oauth4webapi">oauth4webapi</a> for both
      forms; any library that supports a URL as <code>client_id</code>,
      <code>private_key_jwt</code> and PKCE does the same.
      <a href={DEMO}>demo.brig.id</a> shows it running, and
      <a href={DEMO_SOURCE}>its source</a> is the shortest complete example.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="next rise">
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers/apps/">
      Desktop and mobile apps
    </a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers/reference/">
      Reference
    </a>
  </div>
</Developers>
